[0m[0m
Notable C behaviors I had to reproduce rather than fix:[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mbreak[0m inside a [38;5;10mswitch[0m case exits only the switch, so EOF at a sub-prompt causes the menu to be printed one extra time.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mfgets[0m splits lines longer than 255 bytes across successive calls, so an over-long city name leaves its tail to be parsed as the next menu choice.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstrncpy[0m truncates stored names to 63 chars, but the "Added city:" message echoes the untruncated input — so a long name can be added twice without the duplicate check firing.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10msscanf("%d")[0m saturates at [38;5;10mlong[0m range then truncates on assignment to [38;5;10mint[0m, so [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10m-1[0m.[0m[0m
- Nodes are byte-oriented throughout ([38;5;10mVec<u8>[0m), so non-UTF-8 input round-trips like C.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdelete_node[0m frees without removing the pointer from the graph. I model the heap with an arena plus a LIFO free-list, which reproduces two observable glibc effects exactly: a freed node's name is clobbered by allocator metadata so lookups report "not found", and re-adding a deleted city reuses the same chunk, making the stale graph entry alias the new node (verified: the graph then lists the city twice).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One line cannot be matched by any implementation: when the program prints a [3mfreed[23m node's name, C emits raw allocator metadata derived from a heap address. That output is ASLR-dependent and differs between two runs of the C binary on the same input — I confirmed C is not byte-identical to itself there. My version prints an empty name, which is one of the values C itself produces. Everything else on those inputs matches.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two caveats worth knowing: the C program aborts with a double-free SIGABRT on those same use-after-free inputs when compiled at [38;5;10m-O1[0m or higher, so I targeted the CMake default build. And [38;5;10m/tmp[0m on this host is shared — another process overwrote my first reference binary mid-run, so I redid all verification in a private directory.[0m[0m
